UI Navigation System:
UI will have the concept of 'Screens'. A Screen will be a single active page, each with its own definition and layout. There will be a stack of screens, where a top-level main screen is shown first, then selecting a submenu pushes the new screen on the stack. Going back pops that screen and shows the previous one. Transitioning between screens disables UI input while the transision is occuring. Transition will have input disable, a UI fade out, push/pop, instantiate the new screen, fade in, renable input. Each Screen definition will include the layout (html, css) and a corresponding c++ Controller file with all setup and event handlers.
States of the backend and UI will be:
* UI Interactive, backend updating but not interactive (Main menu with 3D world animating in the background
* UI Interactive, backend paused and not inteactive (Pause menu)
* UI non-interactive, backend interactive (gameplay with HUD scoreboard timer).

When UI is interactive, no input (keyboard, mouse, gamepad) is passed to the backend, neither normal gameplay nor dev free camera.
When mouse is hovered over DevUi, input is not passed at all to UI nor backend. Dev function keys always remain active.

UI screens will set text fields from a stringDb. There will be a central StringDbManager to which all UI screens will register. If the language is changed and a new underlying set of localized strings is selected, the StringDbManager calls UpdateStrings on all its registered screens, in which each of them queries to get the text for each of their strings. Question, do screens require copies of the text or can they point to the copy owned by the StringDbManager? Also for init, should screens set their text on init, or wait until all screen objects are instantiated and have registered with StringDbManager, then simply call StringDbManager.Refresh?

Should All screens should perform an initial Init on boot, where any one-time init is done so that transitions from one screen to another are seamless, with all screen elements pre-allocated. Consider if this is the best approach, should initial layouts be calculated ahead or when transitioning?

Read the planning workin progress UI reference folde c# docs for possible ideas.


Add a state transition from AppStateBoot to AppStateShowcase. AppStateBoot will have interactable == UI only. After transitioning to AppStateShowcase, the UI will have a HUD and be non-interactable, world will be interactable. InputTrigger::GamepadSelect triggers a pause, which will pause World updating, set interactable == UI and transition to a pause menu. Pause menu back button returns to the previous UI hud and interactable == world. Pausing the world like this will be a perAppState 


